std.crypto.pbkdf2 — PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256
=======================================

PBKDF2 ("Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2") as specified in
[RFC 2898 §5.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2898#section-5.2),
instantiated with HMAC-SHA-256 as the underlying pseudorandom function.
Derives a 32-byte cryptographic key from a low-entropy password and a
salt by iterating an HMAC many times.

The whole point of PBKDF2 is **deliberate slowness**. A user-chosen
password has perhaps 30–50 bits of entropy; an attacker who steals the
password database wants to grind through every plausible password as
fast as possible. By forcing each guess to perform $c$ HMAC-SHA-256
evaluations, PBKDF2 makes the attacker pay exactly that cost per
guess. Increasing $c$ is the primary security knob: typical web-app
deployments choose $c$ between $10^5$ and $10^6$.

The single-block derivation here computes:

$$
\mathrm{DK} = T_1 = U_1 \oplus U_2 \oplus \cdots \oplus U_c
$$

where

$$
\begin{aligned}
U_1 &= \mathrm{HMAC}(P, S \;\Vert\; \texttt{INT\_32\_BE}(1)) \\
U_i &= \mathrm{HMAC}(P, U_{i-1}) \quad \text{for } i > 1
\end{aligned}
$$

with $P$ the password, $S$ the salt, and $c$ the iteration count.
$\texttt{INT\_32\_BE}(1)$ is the four-byte big-endian encoding of the
block index — which is always 1 in this single-block implementation.

Dependencies: `std.crypto.hmac` for the HMAC PRF, `std.mem` for
buffer copies.

    module std.crypto.pbkdf2

    import std.crypto.hmac.{hmac_sha256, hmac_sha256_hw}
    import std.mem.{copy}


Constants
---------

The HMAC-SHA-256 output size, used as the size of each $U_i$ and the
final derived key.

    val HASH_SIZE = 32


`pbkdf2_hmac_sha256` — software derivation
------------------------------------------

`pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password, salt, iterations, out)` derives a
32-byte key into `out`. `iterations` is the cost parameter $c$ — the
caller decides how many HMAC evaluations are acceptable per
verification.

The output buffer must be at least 32 bytes; bytes beyond that are
not touched.

    pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password: []byte, salt: []byte, iterations: int, out: []byte)
        var U: [32]byte
        var T: [32]byte

The salt and the four-byte big-endian block index "1" are concatenated
into a dynamically-sized buffer. Block index 1 is hard-coded because
this implementation produces exactly one HMAC-output-sized block.

        val slen = len(salt)
        val sbuf = (new [slen + 4]byte)[:]
        copy(sbuf, salt)
        sbuf[slen]     = 0u8
        sbuf[slen + 1] = 0u8
        sbuf[slen + 2] = 0u8
        sbuf[slen + 3] = 1u8

First iteration: $U_1 = \mathrm{HMAC}(P, S \;\Vert\; \texttt{INT\_32\_BE}(1))$.
Seed the running XOR accumulator $T$ with $U_1$.

        hmac_sha256(password, sbuf, U[:])
        copy(T[:], U[:])

Iterations $2..c$: each step feeds the previous $U$ back through HMAC
and XORs it into $T$. After the loop, $T = U_1 \oplus U_2 \oplus \cdots \oplus U_c$.

        for i in 1..<iterations
            hmac_sha256(password, U[:], U[:])
            for j in 0..<HASH_SIZE
                T[j] ^= U[j]

        copy(out, T[:])


`pbkdf2_hmac_sha256_hw` — hardware-accelerated derivation
---------------------------------------------------------

Identical structure to `pbkdf2_hmac_sha256`, but routes every HMAC
call through `hmac_sha256_hw` (the SHA-256 MMIO accelerator). The two
functions produce bit-identical output; only which silicon does the
SHA-256 work changes.

    pbkdf2_hmac_sha256_hw(password: []byte, salt: []byte, iterations: int, out: []byte)
        var U: [32]byte
        var T: [32]byte

        val slen = len(salt)
        val sbuf = (new [slen + 4]byte)[:]
        copy(sbuf, salt)
        sbuf[slen]     = 0u8
        sbuf[slen + 1] = 0u8
        sbuf[slen + 2] = 0u8
        sbuf[slen + 3] = 1u8

        hmac_sha256_hw(password, sbuf, U[:])
        copy(T[:], U[:])

        for i in 1..<iterations
            hmac_sha256_hw(password, U[:], U[:])
            for j in 0..<HASH_SIZE
                T[j] ^= U[j]

        copy(out, T[:])


Tests
=====

    import std.debug.{assert}

The test vectors are independently computed against
[RFC 7914 Appendix A](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7914#appendix-A)
and openssl's `pkcs5_pbkdf2_hmac` for cross-verification.


### PBKDF2 with $c = 1$ — one HMAC iteration

Password `"password"`, salt `"salt"`, $c=1$. The minimum-cost case;
useful as a sanity check that the salt-and-INT32 framing is correct.

Expected: `120fb6cf fcf8b32c 43e72252 56c4f837 a86548c9 2ccc3548 0805987c b70be17b`.

    #test
    test_pbkdf2_c1() -> unit
        val password: [8]byte = "password"
        val salt: [4]byte = "salt"
        var out: [32]byte
        pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password[:], salt[:], 1, out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x12, 0x0f, 0xb6, 0xcf, 0xfc, 0xf8, 0xb3, 0x2c, 0x43, 0xe7, 0x22, 0x52, 0x56, 0xc4, 0xf8, 0x37, 0xa8, 0x65, 0x48, 0xc9, 0x2c, 0xcc, 0x35, 0x48, 0x08, 0x05, 0x98, 0x7c, 0xb7, 0x0b, 0xe1, 0x7b]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"c=1 byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### PBKDF2 with $c = 2$ — exercises the XOR-accumulation loop

Same password and salt with $c=2$. The output differs from $c=1$
because the XOR of $U_1 \oplus U_2$ has nothing in common with $U_1$
alone.

Expected: `ae4d0c95 af6b46d3 2d0adff9 28f06dd0 2a303f8e f3c251df d6e2d85a 95474c43`.

    #test
    test_pbkdf2_c2() -> unit
        val password: [8]byte = "password"
        val salt: [4]byte = "salt"
        var out: [32]byte
        pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password[:], salt[:], 2, out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xae, 0x4d, 0x0c, 0x95, 0xaf, 0x6b, 0x46, 0xd3, 0x2d, 0x0a, 0xdf, 0xf9, 0x28, 0xf0, 0x6d, 0xd0, 0x2a, 0x30, 0x3f, 0x8e, 0xf3, 0xc2, 0x51, 0xdf, 0xd6, 0xe2, 0xd8, 0x5a, 0x95, 0x47, 0x4c, 0x43]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"c=2 byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Short password and salt

Password `"pass"` (4 bytes), salt `"sa"` (2 bytes), $c=1$. Verifies
that small inputs do not run off the ends of any internal buffer.

Expected: `ee3128e0 a8ed80ff 7d38f46e 7a97a2b0 a33fc494 189aedaf cd812acc 752f14ca`.

    #test
    test_pbkdf2_short() -> unit
        val password: [4]byte = "pass"
        val salt: [2]byte = "sa"
        var out: [32]byte
        pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password[:], salt[:], 1, out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xee, 0x31, 0x28, 0xe0, 0xa8, 0xed, 0x80, 0xff, 0x7d, 0x38, 0xf4, 0x6e, 0x7a, 0x97, 0xa2, 0xb0, 0xa3, 0x3f, 0xc4, 0x94, 0x18, 0x9a, 0xed, 0xaf, 0xcd, 0x81, 0x2a, 0xcc, 0x75, 0x2f, 0x14, 0xca]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"short byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### PBKDF2 with $c = 100$ — many-iteration vector

A higher iteration count exercises the inner loop. Runtime scales
linearly with $c$, so $c = 100$ takes about 100 HMACs ≈ 100 × 2 × SHA-256.

Expected: `07e69971 80cf7f12 904f0410 0d405d34 888fdf62 af6d506a 0ecc23b1 96fe99d8`.

    #test
    test_pbkdf2_c100() -> unit
        val password: [8]byte = "password"
        val salt: [4]byte = "salt"
        var out: [32]byte
        pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password[:], salt[:], 100, out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x07, 0xe6, 0x99, 0x71, 0x80, 0xcf, 0x7f, 0x12, 0x90, 0x4f, 0x04, 0x10, 0x0d, 0x40, 0x5d, 0x34, 0x88, 0x8f, 0xdf, 0x62, 0xaf, 0x6d, 0x50, 0x6a, 0x0e, 0xcc, 0x23, 0xb1, 0x96, 0xfe, 0x99, 0xd8]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"c=100 byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Determinism — same inputs produce identical output

    #test
    test_pbkdf2_deterministic() -> unit
        val password: [4]byte = "test"
        val salt: [4]byte = "salt"
        var out1: [32]byte
        var out2: [32]byte
        pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password[:], salt[:], 10, out1[:])
        pbkdf2_hmac_sha256(password[:], salt[:], 10, out2[:])

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out1[i] == out2[i], "deterministic")


Design notes
------------

**Single 32-byte block.** This implementation produces exactly one
HMAC-SHA-256-output-sized block (`HASH_SIZE = 32`). The full RFC 2898
PBKDF2 supports arbitrary derived-key lengths by running the inner
function once per block and concatenating the results. We've not
needed longer outputs; if a future use wants 64-byte derived keys
(e.g. for HKDF-Expand inputs), the outer loop over block indices
would be straightforward to add.

**Dynamic salt buffer.** The `salt ‖ INT_32_BE(1)` buffer is
`(new [slen + 4]byte)`, sized exactly to the salt length, so there is
no fixed cap on `len(salt)`.

**Cost calibration is the caller's job.** Choose `iterations` so a
single derivation takes roughly 100–300 ms on the verifier's hardware
— that matches typical user-facing latency budgets and pushes attack
cost as high as possible without making honest verification annoying.
For SLIX-side password hashing the current default is in the high
tens of thousands; that may want re-tuning on faster CPUs.
